{"type": "tool_result", "name": "skill_view", "tool_call_id": "w01wzHX96eiSySm2QY68iiTXXpf0OPJu", "output": "{\"success\": true, \"name\": \"shopping-sweep\", \"description\": \"Live price sweep: retail + marketplaces, no cached prices.\", \"tags\": [\"shopping\", \"price-sweep\", \"retail\", \"ebay\", \"marketplace\", \"gpu\"], \"related_skills\": [\"laptop-purchase-research\", \"web-search-scraping\", \"facebook-public-data\", \"product-price-monitor\"], \"content\": \"---\\nname: shopping-sweep\\ndescription: \\\"Live price sweep: retail + marketplaces, no cached prices.\\\"\\nversion: 1.1.0\\nauthor: Hermes Agent\\nlicense: MIT\\ncategory: research\\ntriggers:\\n  - shop for\\n  - buy a\\n  - find a deal on\\n  - compare prices\\n  - price check\\n  - marketplace\\n  - facebook marketplace\\n  - ebay\\n  - gpu upgrade\\n  - gpu shopping\\nkeywords:\\n  - shopping\\n  - live price\\n  - sweep\\n  - marketplace\\n  - facebook\\n  - ebay\\n  - offerup\\n  - craigslist\\n  - gpu\\n  - retail\\nmetadata:\\n  hermes:\\n    tags: [shopping, price-sweep, retail, ebay, marketplace, gpu]\\n    related_skills: [laptop-purchase-research, web-search-scraping, facebook-public-data, product-price-monitor]\\nrelated_skills:\\n  - laptop-purchase-research\\n  - web-search-scraping\\n  - facebook-public-data\\n  - product-price-monitor\\n---\\n\\n# Shopping Sweep (Live Multi-Source Price Hunt)\\n\\n## Verification is mandatory: a snippet price is NOT a price\\n\\nA local-model sweep (Qwen3.6-35B-A3B) reported two Dell Latitudes from one seller at an\\nidentical **$276.99**. Both were wrong, and opening the live pages gave **$377.51** and\\n**$359.75** — off by $100.52 and $82.76. It reported a third listing at $265.21 that was\\nactually **$251.95**. The sweep's headline conclusion (\\\"there ARE options under $300\\\") was\\nfalse for the two Dells.\\n\\n**The tells, all present and all ignored by the model:**\\n- Two different listings quoted at an identical price from the same seller.\\n- The model's own output admitted the model numbers were \\\"not confirmed from search\\n  snippets\\\" — i.e. it never opened the pages, despite the task requiring live prices.\\n- Prices arrived from `web_search` result snippets rather than `web_extract` or the browser.\\n\\n**Therefore, for every candidate you intend to report:**\\n\\n1. **Open the listing page itself** (`web_extract`, or the browser for JS/anti-bot pages).\\n   Never quote a price that appears only in a search-results snippet.\\n2. **Read the price from the page**, and also confirm the title and the item specifics\\n   (RAM, storage, screen, condition). A snippet's stated RAM/storage is not verified either.\\n3. **Check for ended/sold listings** — a snippet can surface a sold item at its last price.\\n4. **Verify shipping and returns separately.** \\\"Under $300\\\" means shipped total; a $265\\n   listing with $40 shipping is over budget. Also capture free-shipping/returns status.\\n5. **Distrust duplicate prices.** Two listings at the same figure means one was copied from a\\n   snippet, not read.\\n6. **Re-verify before reporting to the user.** The agent that ran the sweep is not the one\\n   that should sign off on it; a second pass through the live pages is what caught all three\\n   errors here.\\n\\n### Verification recipe (browser path, works on eBay anti-bot)\\n\\nHeadless Chromium via CDP. Note **`--no-sandbox` is required when running as root**, or it\\nfails with `Running as root without --no-sandbox is not supported`:\\n\\n```bash\\nchromium --remote-debugging-port=9222 --remote-allow-origins='*' \\\\\\n  --user-data-dir=/tmp/verify-profile --headless=new --no-sandbox \\\\\\n  --no-first-run --no-default-browser-check --disable-gpu --disable-dev-shm-usage\\n```\\n\\nThen read the price from `div.x-price-primary span.ux-textspans`, plus `h1` for the title and\\n`document.body.innerText` for shipping/returns/ended markers. Allow a few seconds after\\nnavigation; eBay's price node renders late.\\n\\n#### Reading a search results page\\n\\n`web_extract` on a marketplace search URL returns thumbnail images and no prices, so the browser is\\nrequired. Two things make it work: keep the query plain (filter flags like `LH_BIN` return an error\\npage, while category and page-size parameters are safe), and parse the accessibility text dump rather\\nthan CSS class names, which change without notice. Full recipe, the line-window parser, and the\\ncategory id for hardware: `references/ebay-and-used-marketplaces.md`.\\n\\nA price-sorted list is not a shortlist: sorted ascending, the cheapest rows are \\\"FOR PARTS / REPAIR\\\"\\ncards and vague titles naming several different models at once. Read the condition line and the title\\nbefore quoting anything, and treat a \\\"very good condition\\\" outlier at half the market price as bait\\nuntil its item page confirms otherwise.\\n\\nShipping on an item page frequently does not render into `innerText` at all. When it does not, report\\nthe item price and say shipping is unconfirmed. An invented shipping figure is what turns a real\\nunder-budget find into a false claim.\\n\\n- **When eBay's own sold filter walls, a sold-price tracker is the fallback, and its as-of date is\\n  part of the clai...", "duration_ms": 314, "is_error": false, "timestamp": 1793510990443}
